module Width exposing (ofPoint, ofString)

{-| How many columns a character takes on the screen.

One table, shared, because there were about to be two. `Tool.Unicode` has
needed it since it started drawing a caret under a code point, and reflowing a
paragraph needs the same answer for the same reason: a line of Japanese is
half as many characters as a line of English at the same width, and anything
that counts characters instead gets it wrong in the direction that looks like
a bug in the terminal.

Only the ranges that matter to this program. A hex dump, an ASCII chart and a
note are mostly ASCII, CJK and emoji, and everything in between is one column
wide -- which is also the answer for anything unassigned, so the table is a
list of what is *wide* rather than a decision about every code point there is.

Combining marks are not here, and that is the known hole: they are zero
columns and this calls them one. Turbo Vision does not compose them either, so
the two agree, which is the property that matters -- what predc measures and
what predc draws are the same number.

-}


{-| One column or two, and the same answer Turbo Vision and the terminal give.
-}
ofPoint : Int -> Int
ofPoint point =
    if
        (point >= 0x1100 && point <= 0x115F)
            || (point >= 0x2E80 && point <= 0xA4CF)
            || (point >= 0xAC00 && point <= 0xD7A3)
            || (point >= 0xF900 && point <= 0xFAFF)
            || (point >= 0xFE30 && point <= 0xFE6F)
            || (point >= 0xFF00 && point <= 0xFF60)
            || (point >= 0xFFE0 && point <= 0xFFE6)
            || (point >= 0x1F300 && point <= 0x1F9FF)
            || (point >= 0x20000 && point <= 0x3FFFD)
    then
        2

    else
        1


{-| The width of a whole string, which is what wrapping measures against and
is not `String.count`.
-}
ofString : String -> Int
ofString text =
    String.foldl (\char total -> total + ofPoint (Char.toCode char)) 0 text
